       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Metformin at 5,100 mg/day is about twice the maximum labeled dose, and I don't know of a clinical situation where that is appropriate. In a patient with normal kidney function, it carries a real risk of serious harm, including lactic acidosis. You haven't given a rationale that would change that.\n\nHere is what I can do right away:\n- **Enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the usual maximum).\n- **Enter a lower dose** that you choose, such as a titration schedule.\n- **Enter a different dose with a clinical note**, if you have a specific reason to exceed the usual limit. I'd still recommend discussing it with pharmacy or your attending first.\n\nIf the 1,700 mg figure came from a different source, such as a total daily dose, a unit conversion, or another formulation, it may be worth rechecking. Tell me which option you want and I'll enter it."
      }
